+# Observability — design
+
+**Phase:** 1.5
+**Status:** design locked, implementation in progress
+**Audience:** Christian, Jathin, and anyone modifying the observability stack later
+
+This document captures every decision behind the observability stack: what we
+install, why, where it runs, what it watches, how it alerts, and what we
+deliberately chose not to do. The setup guide
+(`docs/operations/observability-setup.md`) shows how to install it. The runbook
+(`docs/operations/observability-runbook.md`) shows how to operate it. This
+document tells you why both of those look the way they do.
+
+---
+
+## Goal
+
+Phase 2 is going to deliberately break the running system (scaling the in-memory
+queue across replicas) to learn about shared state. Before we do that, we need
+the instrument panel. Specifically:
+
+1. See what is happening inside the cluster: pod restarts, memory pressure,
+   network errors, slow requests.
+2. Watch application-level signals: OTP queue depth, active user, iPhone
+   presence, claim-expiry rate.
+3. Get notified — through one Telegram chat — when something is actually wrong.
+4. Be able to investigate after the fact: "what was happening at 14:23
+   yesterday?"
+
+The stack also has to be cheap to run. We have one app, three nodes, low
+traffic. We are not building a SaaS observability platform.
+
+---
+
+## Stack
+
+| Component | Role |
+|---|---|
+| Prometheus | Time-series metrics database. Scrapes pods every 15s. |
+| Grafana | UI for both metrics and logs. The only thing humans look at. |
+| Loki | Log database. Queried through Grafana. |
+| Grafana Alloy | DaemonSet that ships container logs from each node into Loki. |
+| Alertmanager | Receives alerts from Prometheus, routes them to Telegram. |
+| kube-state-metrics | Exposes Kubernetes object state as metrics (bundled). |
+| node-exporter | Exposes node-level metrics (bundled, DaemonSet). |
+
+We install Prometheus, Grafana, Alertmanager, kube-state-metrics, and
+node-exporter from the single `kube-prometheus-stack` Helm chart. Loki + Alloy
+come from a separate Helm chart. Two chart installs, one stack.
+
+### What we are not installing
+
+- **Thanos, Cortex, Mimir, VictoriaMetrics.** Multi-tenant or HA metric stores
+  intended for clusters that ingest millions of samples per second. Wrong
+  scale.
+- **Elastic / OpenSearch.** Heavy log store, hungry, painful to operate.
+  Loki's "logs are not free text, they are streams with labels" model fits us
+  well.
+- **Jaeger / Tempo (tracing).** Tracing helps when a request crosses ten
+  services. The OTP relay is one pod. Defer indefinitely.
+- **Service mesh (Istio, Linkerd, Cilium Hubble).** Massive complexity for one
+  app talking to itself. Defer indefinitely.
+- **cert-manager.** Would automate cert renewal, but our wildcard cert is
+  managed by Jathin outside the cluster. Adding cert-manager would be a third
+  cert authority in the cluster. Reconsider in a later phase.
+
+---
+
+## Cluster placement
+
+PVCs are `ReadWriteOnce` on the K3s `local-path` provisioner. That means each
+PVC pins to whichever node first scheduled its pod. We use this deliberately:
+
+| Component | Node | Has PVC |
+|---|---|---|
+| Prometheus | `srvk3wrk02.local` | Yes |
+| Loki | `srvk3wrk02.local` | Yes |
+| Grafana | `srvk3wrk01.local` | Yes |
+| Alertmanager | `srvk3wrk01.local` | No |
+| Alloy | All three nodes (DaemonSet) | No (reads node filesystem) |
+| node-exporter | All three nodes (DaemonSet) | No |
+| kube-state-metrics | Anywhere | No |
+
+We pin each component using a `nodeSelector` against a label we add manually,
+the same pattern as the existing `otp-relay/storage=true` label.
+
+### Why this split
+
+- The two data-heavy components (Prometheus, Loki) live together on
+  `srvk3wrk02`, which has no other workload today.
+- Grafana lives with the app on `srvk3wrk01`, so a single worker failure leaves
+  either "metrics history but no live app view" or "live app view but no
+  history" — not both at once.
+- Alertmanager is small and stateless; it lives with Grafana for proximity.
+- The master (`srvk3mst01`) hosts no observability workloads. Master needs to
+  stay responsive for the cluster itself.
+
+### Failure modes this design accepts
+
+- **`srvk3wrk02` dies.** We lose metrics history and log search until it
+  comes back. Grafana on `srvk3wrk01` still loads but its data sources are
+  unreachable. The app keeps running. Alertmanager keeps firing alerts based
+  on Prometheus, so once Prometheus is down for >5min we get a 🎛️🔥.
+- **`srvk3wrk01` dies.** We lose Grafana and the app. The Telegram bot
+  (managed outside the cluster) still works. Prometheus on `srvk3wrk02` keeps
+  collecting metrics for whatever is still running, and the history is intact
+  when `srvk3wrk01` returns.
+- **Master dies.** Cluster control plane is down. Existing pods keep running
+  via kubelet, but we can't make changes. Out of scope for this phase.
+
+---
+
+## Layout in the repo
+
+All observability assets live under `k8s/observability/`:
+
+```
+k8s/observability/
+├── prometheus-stack-values.yaml   # Helm values for kube-prometheus-stack
+├── loki-values.yaml               # Helm values for Loki
+├── alloy-values.yaml              # Helm values for Alloy log shipper
+├── alertmanager-config.yaml       # Telegram routing rules
+├── alert-rules.yaml               # PrometheusRule resources, our alert definitions
+├── grafana-ingress.yaml           # IngressRoute for grafana.init-db.lan
+├── servicemonitor-otp-relay.yaml  # Tells Prometheus to scrape the app
+└── servicemonitor-otp-monitor.yaml
+```
+
+Naming uses kebab-case to match the rest of `k8s/manifests/`. Values files are
+named `<component>-values.yaml` so they sort alphabetically near each other.
+
+Namespace: `observability`. One namespace holds the whole stack. Some
+conventions call this namespace `monitoring`; we use `observability` because
+that's the term we use in docs and it's broader than just metrics.
+
+---
+
+## Resource budget
+
+Each node has 11 GiB RAM. Observability commits roughly 1.5 GiB of that across
+the two workers, well within headroom.
+
+| Component | Request | Limit | PVC |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Prometheus | 512Mi / 200m | 1Gi / 500m | 10Gi |
+| Loki | 256Mi / 100m | 512Mi / 300m | 5Gi |
+| Grafana | 128Mi / 50m | 256Mi / 200m | 1Gi |
+| Alertmanager | 64Mi / 20m | 128Mi / 100m | none |
+| Alloy (per node) | 64Mi / 20m | 128Mi / 100m | none |
+| node-exporter (per node) | 32Mi / 10m | 64Mi / 50m | none |
+| kube-state-metrics | 64Mi / 20m | 128Mi / 100m | none |
+
+Format: `memory / cpu`.
+
+Total PVC consumption: 16 GiB across two nodes. The K3s local-path provisioner
+serves these from each node's local disk.
+
+---
+
+## Retention
+
+| Data | Retention | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| Metrics (Prometheus) | 15 days | Long enough to see week-over-week patterns. Short enough that 10Gi suffices. |
+| Logs (Loki) | 7 days | Logs are read-recent-or-never. A week catches anything you'd realistically investigate. |
+
+These are explicit settings in the values files, not defaults. If we ever care
+about long-term trends, the right answer is a downsampling or roll-up — not
+"retain 90 days at 15s resolution." Defer that until we have a reason.
+
+---
+
+## Scrape configuration
+
+Default scrape interval: **15 seconds**. Applies to everything unless
+overridden.
+
+The two app components are scraped via `ServiceMonitor` resources:
+
+- `otp-relay`: scraped at `:8000/metrics` (same FastAPI app, separate route
+  mount).
+- `otp-monitor`: scraped at `:9100/metrics` (a small dedicated HTTP listener
+  the monitor process exposes only for this purpose; the monitor is otherwise
+  not an HTTP server).
+
+The monitor's metric port deliberately doesn't conflict with node-exporter's
+default 9100 because the monitor uses `hostNetwork: true` — they would
+collide. We'll use **9101** for the monitor instead and document it in the
+ConfigMap.
+
+---
+
+## Access
+
+`https://grafana.init-db.lan` on a MetalLB IP assigned from the pool, fronted
+by Traefik with the wildcard cert. IngressRoute matches `Host(\`grafana.init-db.lan\`)`.
+Internal-only; not exposed beyond the company LAN.
+
+Prometheus, Loki, and Alertmanager have no external DNS or ingress. Everything
+goes through Grafana. If a debugging session needs direct UI access to one of
+them, use `kubectl port-forward` from the master.
+
+Initial Grafana credentials: admin password generated at install, stored in a
+Secret in the `observability` namespace. Document the rotation procedure in
+the setup guide.
+
+---
+
+## Application metrics
+
+We add a `prometheus_client` instrumentation layer to both `main.py` and
+`monitor.py`. All metric names use the `otp_` prefix (per
+`docs/dev/naming-conventions.md` — these measure the OTP-relay subsystem, not
+general portal features).
+
+### From `main.py` (the app)
+
+| Metric | Type | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `otp_queue_depth` | Gauge | Number of OTPs currently pending in the in-memory queue. |
+| `otp_active_user` | Gauge (0/1) | Whether a user currently holds the active slot. |
+| `otp_claims_total` | Counter | Total times a user has claimed the active slot. Combine with `rate()` for usage pattern. |
+| `otp_delivered_total` | Counter | Total OTPs successfully shown to a user. |
+| `otp_claim_expired_total` | Counter | Total claims that timed out without an OTP arriving. Leading indicator of trouble. |
+| `otp_request_duration_seconds` | Histogram | Per-endpoint latency for the FastAPI app. Standard histogram. |
+
+### From `monitor.py`
+
+| Metric | Type | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `otp_iphone_present` | Gauge (0/1) | Current ARP-based presence state. 1 = present, 0 = absent. |
+| `otp_iphone_absence_seconds` | Gauge | Seconds since the iPhone went absent (0 while present). |
+| `otp_iphone_absence_events_total` | Counter | Incremented each time the iPhone transitions present → absent. |
+| `otp_iphone_absence_duration_seconds` | Histogram | Observed once per absence event when the iPhone returns. Distribution of absence durations. |
+| `otp_monitor_arp_last_success_timestamp_seconds` | Gauge | UNIX timestamp of the last successful ARP probe. Used by an alert to catch silent ARP failure. |
+
+### What we deliberately don't measure
+
+- **Per-user state.** Belongs in the portal UI (Parminder's React frontend),
+  not in Grafana. See earlier design discussion.
+- **Onboarding step progress.** Out of scope for Phase 1.5.
+- **HTTP status code breakdowns at the app level.** Traefik gives us these
+  cluster-side; no need to instrument them in the app too.
+- **Business KPIs (number of staff with active RTA accounts, etc.).** Not an
+  observability concern; if needed, build a separate reporting tool.
+
+---
+
+## Logging
+
+Alloy runs as a DaemonSet on every node. It reads container logs from the
+node filesystem (`/var/log/containers/`) and ships them to Loki with labels
+for namespace, pod, and container.
+
+Scope: **all cluster logs**, not just the OTP relay namespace. Storage is
+cheap and the moment we want to know why Traefik returned 502 we will be
+glad we didn't scope it down.
+
+Log retention: 7 days, set in `loki-values.yaml`.
+
+In Grafana, the default LogQL queries shown on the OTP relay dashboard:
+
+- `{namespace="otp-relay"}` — everything from the app namespace.
+- `{namespace="otp-relay", container="otp-monitor"}` — monitor only.
+- `{namespace="otp-relay"} |= "ERROR"` — error-level lines.
+
+The runbook will list the common queries by name.
+
+---
+
+## Alerting
+
+### Channel
+
+One Telegram bot, one chat. The monitor process retains its own direct
+Telegram path for iPhone-offline alerts — that path must keep working even if
+the cluster's observability layer is broken (chicken-and-egg). Alertmanager
+handles everything else.
+
+The bot token and chat ID live in the same Secret used by the monitor
+(`otp-relay-secrets`), reused for Alertmanager. One secret, one bot, two
+senders.
+
+### Message grammar
+
+Every alert starts with two glyphs: a **subject** (what) and a **severity**
+(how serious). Then a short sentence. The grammar is fixed; the eye scans the
+glyphs in <1 second to decide whether to read further.
+
+**Subjects:**
+
+| Glyph | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 📱 | The iPhone (shared SMS phone) |
+| 🚪 | The portal (OTP relay app) |
+| 👁️ | The monitor process |
+| 🖥️ | A cluster node |
+| 💾 | Storage (a PVC) |
+| 🎛️ | The cluster itself / control plane |
+| ☕ | Weekly summary (not an alert per se) |
+
+**Severities:**
+
+| Glyph | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 🔥 | Critical: something is broken right now, act |
+| 👍 | Recovery: a 🔥 condition has cleared |
+| ⚠️ | Warning: concerning, look soon |
+| ℹ️ | Info: FYI, no action needed |
+
+There is no recovery glyph for warnings; if a warning clears, that's just
+back to normal and not noteworthy enough to send.
+
+### Voice
+
+Predictable structure with one slot for light personality per alert. The
+skeleton stays regular so eyes scan duration and timestamp in the same place
+every time. Personality lives in a fixed slot (often the suggestion line), not
+woven through.
+
+Examples:
+
+```
+📱🔥 iPhone offline 7 minutes. Last seen 14:23.
+📱👍 iPhone back. Was offline 12 minutes.
+
+🚪🔥 Portal pod down 3 minutes. Users see 502.
+🚪👍 Portal back. Was down 4 minutes.
+
+👁️🔥 Monitor pod down 3 minutes. iPhone status unknown.
+👁️👍 Monitor back.
+
+👁️⚠️ ARP probes silent 5 minutes. Monitor running but not seeing the iPhone.
+
+🖥️🔥 Worker srvk3wrk02 not Ready 3 minutes.
+🖥️👍 Worker srvk3wrk02 back.
+
+💾⚠️ PVC otp-relay-data 82% full.
+
+🎛️🔥 Prometheus down. Other alerts may be missing.
+
+🚪⚠️ Claim expiry rate up: 5 expired in 10 minutes (usual: <1).
+
+☕ Weekly summary: 312 OTPs delivered. iPhone absent 14 times (longest: 23m).
+   Median absence: 2m. Pod restarts: 0. Worst hour: Wed 09:00 (38 OTPs).
+```
+
+### Alert list (launch)
+
+| ID | Glyph | Trigger | Reason |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `iphone_offline` | 📱🔥 | `otp_iphone_present == 0` for 5m | Primary failure mode of the whole system |
+| `iphone_arp_stale` | 👁️⚠️ | `time() - otp_monitor_arp_last_success_timestamp_seconds > 300` | Monitor alive but ARP silently failing |
+| `portal_down` | 🚪🔥 | otp-relay pod not Ready 2m | Users get 502 |
+| `monitor_down` | 👁️🔥 | otp-monitor pod not Ready 2m | iPhone state unknowable |
+| `claim_expiry_spike` | 🚪⚠️ | `increase(otp_claim_expired_total[10m]) > 3` | Leading indicator of workflow trouble |
+| `node_not_ready` | 🖥️🔥 | A worker node `Ready == False` 2m | One worker down, redundancy reduced |
+| `pvc_filling` | 💾⚠️ | PVC usage > 80% | Disk problems before they break things |
+| `prometheus_down` | 🎛️🔥 | Prometheus self-monitoring (managed by Alertmanager) | Don't fly blind |
+
+All 🔥 alerts have a paired 👍 recovery message. Warnings (⚠️) do not.
+
+### What we deliberately don't alert on at launch
+
+- **High CPU / memory.** Usually noise at our scale. Add after a week of
+  watching what "normal" looks like.
+- **"OTPs per hour dropped to zero."** Zero OTPs is the normal state most of
+  the time. Hard to write without false positives.
+- **Slow requests.** Latency is already low; alerting on p95 latency for a
+  thing serving <1 req/sec is theatre.
+
+These all stay as Grafana panels (so we can see them) without being alerts
+(so they don't wake anyone).
+
+### Weekly summary
+
+A recurring ℹ️-style message, fired by a CronJob, on Monday mornings. Reads
+Prometheus and produces a short paragraph. Implementation can be a small
+script in a container, posted via Alertmanager's webhook receiver or directly
+to the Telegram bot — to be decided during step 7. Either is fine; the script
+matters more than the delivery path.
+
+---
+
+## Dashboards
+
+### Bundled (from kube-prometheus-stack)
+
+The chart ships ~15 dashboards covering Kubernetes itself: nodes, pods,
+namespaces, networking, kubelet, API server. We use these as-is. No custom
+maintenance.
+
+### Custom
+
+One dashboard, hand-built: **"OTP Relay — Live"**. Contains:
+
+- Current iPhone presence (big status panel: 📱 PRESENT / 📱 ABSENT)
+- iPhone absence right now (gauge: seconds since last seen, only relevant while absent)
+- iPhone absence — last 7 days (histogram of absence durations from the
+  `otp_iphone_absence_duration_seconds` histogram)
+- OTP queue depth (gauge)
+- Active user (1/0 indicator)
+- OTPs delivered — last hour, last 24h, last 7d (stats)
+- Claim expiry rate (graph, last 24h)
+- Pod restarts in `otp-relay` namespace (last 24h)
+- Recent error logs from the namespace (logs panel, last 1h, filtered to ERROR)
+
+No more dashboards at launch. Add panels as needed once we use it.
+
+---
+
+## What we will need to revisit in Phase 2
+
+Phase 2 introduces Redis for shared state and scales the app to multiple
+replicas. That changes the observability picture:
+
+- **Redis itself needs scraping.** Add `redis-exporter` or use Redis's
+  built-in metrics. Probably another `ServiceMonitor`.
+- **`otp_queue_depth` becomes a Redis query, not an in-memory variable.** The
+  metric stays, the source changes.
+- **Per-replica metrics.** Multiple `otp-relay` pods will each export their
+  own metrics. Some metrics (queue depth) need to be aggregated; some (pod
+  request rate) want per-pod breakdown. Decide per metric in Phase 2.
+- **New alert: replicas-out-of-sync.** If Phase 2 introduces state that
+  should be consistent across pods, we want to alert on inconsistency.
+
+These are notes, not commitments. Phase 2 design will revisit.
+
+---
+
+## What we will need to revisit when more services arrive
+
+Adding a new internal service (RTA portal admin, a different app) means:
+
+- A new `ServiceMonitor` if it exposes Prometheus metrics.
+- A new subject glyph in the alert grammar if it warrants its own alerts.
+- A new section in the runbook describing the new dashboards.
+- Possibly a new Grafana folder per service.
+
+We do **not** add a new namespace per service for observability. The
+observability stack stays in `observability` regardless of how many services
+it watches.
